cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or build on these experiments, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "ITASORL: Incidental Detection of Surrogate Worlds by a From-Scratch Digital Organism"
authors:
  - family-names: "Tate"
    given-names: "Levy"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0009-1337-0709"
repository-code: "https://github.com/iLevyTate/ItaSoRL"
license: MIT
version: 1.0.0
abstract: >-
  ITASORL tests whether a self-supervised digital organism, trained with no
  imported semantic knowledge, incidentally encodes whether its world is an
  authentic base simulation or a generative surrogate. Hand-tuned dynamics
  artifacts (L2) are readily detectable by a noiseless privileged oracle but are
  not encoded by the agent (robust negative; at the L3 gate's detector noise the
  L2 oracle reads 0.62 to 0.65, so the matched contrast is L1 against L3); a
  learned-dynamics fingerprint surrogate (L3) is incidentally encoded by
  survival-trained agents carrying a next-observation auxiliary, with a
  behavior-independent component that clears the pre-registered bar. Neither
  objective alone reaches the bar (0.601 without the auxiliary, device-controlled).
  Survival-specificity holds at two of three tested fingerprints, including an
  independently trained one that falls under the bar (0.639). A
  cross-recipe probe shows the signal generalizes across surrogate families
  (it also reads a random-Fourier-features law fit on the same data); a
  same-recipe held-out probe adds capacity-variant robustness in one direction
  only (subtle-to-coarse passes, the frozen reverse fails). A
  common-garden control, re-scored with a corrected estimator, passes the frozen
  rule on both directions (forward 0.666, reverse 0.684), so the signal carries a
  modest persistent stored world-identity component the policy also expresses
  reactively, not purely reactive tracking of the felt dynamics
  (see FINDINGS section 10.6.1).
keywords:
  - artificial-life
  - reinforcement-learning
  - self-supervised-learning
  - simulation-hypothesis
  - world-models
